<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mainz</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mainz is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and one of the oldest episcopal seats north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. Its archbishop was one of the seven prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and served as arch-chancellor of Germany. Mainz is also known as the city where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-gutenberg">Johannes Gutenberg</a> developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/movable-type">movable type</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mainz.jpg" alt="Mainz" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: salomon10 from Germany · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mainzer_Dom_Blaue_Stunde_(37539430014).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
